## 3.1 What Is Software Engineering?
 
> **Big idea:** Software engineering is the disciplined, teamwork-based way of designing, building, testing, and maintaining software so that it is dependable and affordable to change.
 
**Analogy:** Programming is like cooking a meal for yourself. Software engineering is like running a restaurant kitchen: you need a plan, a team, quality checks, and a process that works every day.
 
## 3.2 The Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC)
 
Every project moves through roughly these stages:
 
1. **Requirements:** Find out what users actually need.
2. **Design:** Plan how the software will be structured.
3. **Implementation:** Write the code.
4. **Testing:** Check that it works and find bugs.
5. **Deployment:** Release it to users.
6. **Maintenance:** Fix problems and add improvements over time.
## 3.3 Development Models
 
| Model | How it works | Best when |
|---|---|---|
| **Waterfall** | Finish each stage completely before starting the next | Requirements are clear and won't change |
| **Agile** | Build in short cycles (sprints), get feedback, and adjust | Requirements change or are uncertain |
| **Iterative / Incremental** | Build the software in repeating rounds, adding more each time | You want working versions early |
 
**Agile in one line:** Build a little, show it, learn, improve, repeat.
 
## 3.4 Testing
 
- **Unit testing:** Tests small individual pieces of code.
- **Integration testing:** Tests that the pieces work together.
- **System testing:** Tests the whole program.
- **Acceptance testing:** Users or clients confirm it meets their needs.
- **Regression testing:** Re-tests after changes to make sure nothing old broke.
## 3.5 Version Control
 
Version control tracks every change to your code, lets teams work together without overwriting each other, and lets you go back to older versions.
 
- **Git** is the most common tool. **GitHub** is a website that hosts Git projects (like the repository in your screenshot).
- Basic terms: **repository** (project storage), **commit** (a saved snapshot of changes), **branch** (a separate line of work), **merge** (combining branches).
## 3.6 Good Design Principles
 
- **Modularity:** Break the system into small, separate parts.
- **Abstraction:** Hide complicated details behind a simple interface.
- **DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself):** Avoid duplicating code or logic.
- **Separation of concerns:** Each part should have one clear job.
- **Documentation:** Write down how things work so others can follow.
## 3.7 Teamwork and Roles
 
Common roles include **product owner/manager** (decides what to build), **developer** (writes code), **tester/QA** (checks quality), **designer** (user experience), and **project manager** (keeps work on track).
